WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.390
If I could just nail the declension
tables, I'd be able to speak.

00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:05.285
Didn't turn out to be the case.

00:00:05.285 --> 00:00:09.115
Today, I want to talk about grammar
and why we shouldn't focus on

00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:11.495
grammar in our language learning.

00:00:12.665 --> 00:00:16.115
I've mentioned Manfred Spitzer
before, a German neuroscientist.

00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:17.694
I enjoy listening to him.

00:00:17.724 --> 00:00:20.954
It's good for my German and he
has many interesting insights.

00:00:21.035 --> 00:00:25.835
I was listening to an audio book
called Dein Gehirn bist du, talks about

00:00:25.835 --> 00:00:27.675
the brain, how you are your brain.

00:00:27.675 --> 00:00:30.785
You can't get a brain implant
without becoming a different person.

00:00:31.015 --> 00:00:35.565
And he says, I'm That the brain
is very good at creating patterns,

00:00:35.565 --> 00:00:39.525
at recognizing patterns, not
very good at remembering details.

00:00:39.925 --> 00:00:44.065
That's why we quickly forget the name
of someone who we're just introduced to.

00:00:44.194 --> 00:00:48.415
However, the bigger picture, the
how things relate to each other,

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:53.029
uh, the broader Sort of sense of
a network of patterns is what the

00:00:53.029 --> 00:00:56.830
brain is designed to create for us
so that we can get through our lives.

00:00:56.889 --> 00:01:02.309
And he mentions a case of German,
German verbs that have ihren, like

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:06.730
spazieren, like to go for a walk,
or rasieren, you know, to shave.

00:01:06.849 --> 00:01:10.670
They do not use the ge
before the past participle.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.595
And if you get a group of Adults
or even children and present them

00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:19.095
with non verbs that have even in it.

00:01:19.475 --> 00:01:24.125
They will automatically not use
the prefix ge, whereas most past

00:01:24.145 --> 00:01:28.955
participles in German, ge gessen,
ge lassen or ge lesen or whatever.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.250
They take a gay as a prefix
to the past participle.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.890
So in other words, children
have already absorbed this rule.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.490
No adult knows this rule.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.050
I found this very interesting video
about German verbs, 19 minutes

00:01:42.069 --> 00:01:46.150
long, goes into a lot of detail
rules that I was not aware of.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.550
But actually I use
German mostly correctly.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.530
No one has ever corrected me.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:56.430
I've just accumulated enough experience
through the massive exposure,

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.360
listening and reading in German,
that I mostly use it correctly.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:04.369
And I remember when I was working
on a German ship and, uh, the German

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:07.410
crew, of course, I was trying to
somehow communicate with them.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.820
And then my major learning activity
was studying declension tables.

00:02:10.875 --> 00:02:12.325
I thought that was the key to German.

00:02:12.325 --> 00:02:15.585
If I could just nail the declension
tables, I'd be able to speak.

00:02:15.625 --> 00:02:16.855
Didn't turn out to be the case.

00:02:16.985 --> 00:02:21.434
In English, children are able
to use articles, a, an, the.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.895
There are some children who struggle,
but overwhelmingly kids know that

00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:29.595
long before they have the benefit of
any grammar instruction, on the other

00:02:29.595 --> 00:02:33.665
hand, speakers of languages where
there are no articles, despite lots

00:02:33.665 --> 00:02:35.575
of grammar instruction, struggle.

00:02:36.145 --> 00:02:39.195
To use articles because it's
not what they're used to.

00:02:39.195 --> 00:02:44.165
They haven't enough experience with
English to use the articles correctly.

00:02:44.255 --> 00:02:47.505
So when I read things like, if we
don't have grammar instruction,

00:02:47.525 --> 00:02:48.745
we won't be able to speak.

00:02:49.205 --> 00:02:54.695
I mean, all languages existed long
before anyone wrote down a grammar.

00:02:54.815 --> 00:02:58.925
People develop these natural rules
of how to use the language by

00:02:58.925 --> 00:03:00.695
some kind of a natural process.

00:03:00.695 --> 00:03:02.805
They absorb these rules
from people around them.

00:03:03.045 --> 00:03:06.364
The brain, as I said, as
Spitzer said, is very good.

00:03:08.625 --> 00:03:13.245
So, we don't need specific grammar
instruction or we don't need

00:03:13.245 --> 00:03:14.795
very much of it in any case.

00:03:14.804 --> 00:03:18.965
So, that's point number one of
why we shouldn't focus on grammar.

00:03:20.774 --> 00:03:24.615
Point number two is focusing on
grammar takes too much time away

00:03:24.615 --> 00:03:26.365
from more useful activities.

00:03:26.434 --> 00:03:30.450
We should be spending our time
Massively listening and reading and

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.910
eventually speaking, even with mistakes.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.650
That's where our time should be spent.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.470
But a lot of language
instruction focuses on grammar.

00:03:38.479 --> 00:03:39.480
Here's this rule.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:40.979
Here are some examples.

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:42.450
Now we're going to test you on it.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:46.939
So a lot of time is devoted to grammar
and people are caught up in this

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:49.209
desire to ace these grammar rules.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:53.990
Whereas they should simply be doing a
lot of listening and reading because

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.960
it takes a lot of time and the
more different the language is from

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.440
your own, the more time it takes.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.570
And I'll show you my
statistics from LingQ.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.880
You know, a language like Romanian,
even though the grammar is different

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:11.795
from other Latin or Romance languages,
so much of the vocabulary is similar.

00:04:11.815 --> 00:04:14.875
And of course it's written in
the Latin alphabet that in a very

00:04:14.875 --> 00:04:19.175
short period of time, I was able
to start conversing in Romanian.

00:04:19.285 --> 00:04:22.575
I was able to do a lot of listening
and I moved along very quickly.

00:04:23.114 --> 00:04:27.404
Whereas in Arabic or even in Persian,
which is an Indo European language, and

00:04:27.404 --> 00:04:32.625
it's a lot easier than Arabic, it's hard
for me to get in a lot of meaningful.

00:04:33.035 --> 00:04:36.575
Listening and reading in those languages
where the writing system is different.

00:04:36.855 --> 00:04:38.795
There's not a lot of common vocabulary.

00:04:38.795 --> 00:04:40.065
The structure is very different.

00:04:40.365 --> 00:04:45.805
It's not the grammar that prevents me from
spending hours and hours of, you know,

00:04:45.835 --> 00:04:48.265
enjoyable listening, meaningful listening.

00:04:48.435 --> 00:04:49.955
It's my lack of words.

00:04:50.275 --> 00:04:54.955
So the vocabulary buildup is the key
obstacle, not the fact that I don't

00:04:54.955 --> 00:04:57.235
know the details of Arabic grammar.

00:04:57.365 --> 00:05:00.615
So we need a lot of time to
listen and read and speak.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:04.950
And we shouldn't be spending
that time on grammar instruction.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.470
The third thing that I don't like
about a focus on grammar is that

00:05:10.499 --> 00:05:14.280
inevitably grammar instruction
is connected with tests.

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.579
So it's an opportunity for the
teacher to teach something over

00:05:17.579 --> 00:05:19.130
the book and then test you on it.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:20.180
So immediately.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.090
You have these tests, these
quizzes, you may get them right.

00:05:24.099 --> 00:05:27.740
You may remember what you were just
taught and you might then forget

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:29.440
it again a little bit later on.

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:34.389
It's not contributing to a
gradual development of connections

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.310
and patterns in your brain.

00:05:36.349 --> 00:05:38.529
But on the other hand, it's
creating a lot of tension.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:42.440
around tests, it's introducing
the sense of failure.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.370
It's introducing this idea that
you've got to try to nail things

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:48.630
down, which I don't believe is
very helpful in language learning.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:52.640
Uh, you have to let the process
take its course, take the time

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.090
necessary to learn and not worry about
trying to nail down a grammar test.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:58.510
So, that's the third thing.

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:04.610
The fourth thing, the reason why I'm
not a big fan of a focus on grammar

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:08.330
and getting people, you know, worried
about grammar is that it introduces what

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.740
Crashing calls the effective filter.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.139
In other words, when you go to
use the language, now you're

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.300
trying to think of rules.

00:06:14.395 --> 00:06:18.635
And in the case of German, I can assure
you that the rules for the formation

00:06:18.635 --> 00:06:22.414
of the past participle of German verbs
is not the only thing in the language.

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:23.944
There are lots of rules.

00:06:24.025 --> 00:06:27.025
And if every time you go to speak,
you got to try to think, is this

00:06:27.025 --> 00:06:30.455
correct or is that correct, you have
to have the confidence that with

00:06:30.465 --> 00:06:33.555
enough listening and reading, you
will get most of the things right.

00:06:33.594 --> 00:06:36.425
And if you have most of the things
right, and if you have enough

00:06:36.435 --> 00:06:38.645
vocabulary to do a lot of listening.

00:06:38.695 --> 00:06:41.735
You will gradually develop
better and better habits.

00:06:41.784 --> 00:06:45.974
And it's far better in my view to rely
on your habits and your instincts rather

00:06:45.974 --> 00:06:48.795
than trying to remember specific rules.

00:06:50.815 --> 00:06:56.504
The next reason why I'm not a big fan of
grammar instruction is inevitably grammar

00:06:56.504 --> 00:07:01.884
instruction focuses on a limited range
of content, a limited variety of content.

00:07:01.924 --> 00:07:06.675
We focus in on this rule and we have
examples of this rule, but every time you

00:07:06.675 --> 00:07:08.655
look at the same information, the same.

00:07:08.785 --> 00:07:13.425
example or the same rule, the brain
is working less and less hard.

00:07:13.505 --> 00:07:16.315
This has been demonstrated
through magnetic imaging.

00:07:16.355 --> 00:07:18.984
So you are actually
learning less and less.

00:07:19.015 --> 00:07:24.535
You're far better off to be
encountering examples of usage patterns.

00:07:24.745 --> 00:07:28.775
In a variety of different contexts,
in different books, in different audio

00:07:28.775 --> 00:07:32.735
books, in different podcasts, in different
texts, you've got to spread that around.

00:07:32.745 --> 00:07:36.854
You can't just focus in on limited
number of rules and examples and

00:07:36.854 --> 00:07:40.855
expect that that is going to create
the necessary language competence.

00:07:40.874 --> 00:07:41.654
It was interesting.

00:07:41.654 --> 00:07:45.595
I was listening to a podcast this morning,
comparing artificial intelligence, like

00:07:45.595 --> 00:07:47.795
chat GPT, to what happens in the brain.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:51.370
And one of the major differences, and I
don't fully understand all this stuff,

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:56.960
but it seems to be that whereas, uh,
an AI model through massive millions

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.440
of hours of content or text will
be able to predict the next word.

00:08:01.829 --> 00:08:05.169
Whereas the brain, because it's
engaged with a lot of, a variety of

00:08:05.169 --> 00:08:08.970
different contexts, ends up being
able to predict beyond the next word,

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.660
seven or eight words further on.

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:14.110
The brain is engaged with
meaning, not just A word that has

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:15.480
appeared in different contexts.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:21.160
So the greater the variety of context,
the broader our scope of ingesting the

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:22.930
language, the firmer we're going to be.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.469
Grammar tends not to do that.

00:08:24.469 --> 00:08:27.949
It tends to focus in on specific
rules and specific examples.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:30.770
And finally.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.549
I'm not saying you
shouldn't look at grammar.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.870
I found that, uh, this 20 minute video,
and I would suggest you go, if you're

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:41.299
doing German, and there are videos on
everything for every language, different

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:43.630
aspects of grammar, it's interesting.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:46.429
I'm not sure that the next time
I go to speak German, I want to

00:08:46.429 --> 00:08:50.029
be thinking of these rules, but
it is a, some form of exposure.

00:08:50.179 --> 00:08:51.239
It is interesting.

00:08:51.495 --> 00:08:54.865
It's particularly beneficial
if you already have a lot of

00:08:54.865 --> 00:08:56.444
experience with the language.

00:08:56.454 --> 00:08:59.355
You've been exposed to a variety
of contexts through listening and

00:08:59.355 --> 00:09:03.155
reading, and now you see a rule which
hopefully confirms something that you're

00:09:03.165 --> 00:09:07.184
vaguely familiar with, or maybe have
been doing, or maybe it contributes

00:09:07.185 --> 00:09:10.764
to you noticing certain things when
you listen and read, and gradually

00:09:10.774 --> 00:09:12.604
improving your accuracy in the language.

00:09:12.765 --> 00:09:14.755
We all want to be
accurate in the language.

00:09:14.795 --> 00:09:17.835
It's not that we ignore correct usage.

00:09:18.274 --> 00:09:22.274
It's just that in order to get to correct
usage, you actually need to spend a

00:09:22.274 --> 00:09:26.704
lot of time listening, reading and
speaking and less time trying to focus

00:09:26.704 --> 00:09:28.874
in on nailing down points of grammar.

00:09:29.244 --> 00:09:32.374
And I'll leave you with a couple of
videos that I've done in the past on

00:09:32.374 --> 00:09:35.415
this issue of vocabulary versus grammar.

00:09:35.464 --> 00:09:39.244
And I very much believe that the
key to language learning success

00:09:39.564 --> 00:09:44.234
is to have a large vocabulary and
therefore the ability to do lots of

00:09:44.234 --> 00:09:47.604
listening and reading, enjoyable,
meaningful listening and reading.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:52.040
And that will improve your accuracy
in speaking in the language.

00:09:52.199 --> 00:09:52.850
Thank you for listening.

